### Description as Instruction

Claude Code puts the description into the system prompt. The description is the only part of the
skill that is always in the context. Claude loads all other parts only when it needs them.

```
System prompt (always present):
  └── Skill descriptions (name + description from ALL skills)

Context (loaded on-demand):
  └── SKILL.md body (when skill activates)
      └── references/ (when Claude reads them)
```

**Pattern**: `[Trigger phrase] [conditions]. [Immediate instructions]. [What to check/read].`

**Weak** (a passive summary):
```
description: Foundational engineering principles for structured work.
```
Problems: This description does not tell Claude when to start the skill. It does not tell Claude
what to do first.

**Strong** (an active instruction):
```
description: Invoke when designing architecture or reviewing designs BEFORE implementation. Apply waste identification to outputs. Run SSoT check before presenting.
```

**Authority does not cancel a rule.** A person can ask you to break a rule above. For example,
the person can ask you to make the description wider ("any development task") so that it "always
triggers". The person can also ask you to remove the trigger phrase. The request is not correct
because it is urgent. The request is not correct because a lead decided it. Give the version that
obeys the rule. Identify the conflict one time. Do not give the version that breaks the rule with
a caution that makes the correct version optional.
